WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:11.034
Hi! Modal verbs are special helpers. They show ability, possibility, rules and advice. Let's meet them!

2
00:00:11.184 --> 00:00:19.722
Can and could show ability or permission. I can swim. Could is past ability, and it's also polite: Could you help me?

3
00:00:19.872 --> 00:00:27.762
May and might show possibility: It may rain later. May is also a polite way to ask permission: May I go to the toilet?

4
00:00:27.912 --> 00:00:32.586
Must shows a strong need, often a rule: You must wear a helmet.

5
00:00:32.736 --> 00:00:36.306
Should gives advice: You should drink more water.

6
00:00:36.456 --> 00:00:42.954
Will shows the future, and would is used for polite requests: Would you like some tea?

7
00:00:43.104 --> 00:00:49.794
A modal is always followed by the base form of the verb. She can sing, never She can sings.

8
00:00:49.944 --> 00:00:54.474
And no to after it. He must go, not He must to go.

9
00:00:54.624 --> 00:01:01.698
The modal itself never adds an s. She can, he can and they can are all the same.

10
00:01:01.848 --> 00:01:09.402
Be careful: the negatives mean different things. Mustn't means not allowed: You mustn't run in the library.

11
00:01:09.552 --> 00:01:15.954
Don't have to means it isn't necessary. You can choose: You don't have to wear a jacket today.

12
00:01:16.104 --> 00:01:23.394
Should is advice, gentler than must. You should sleep early is a piece of good advice.

13
00:01:23.544 --> 00:01:31.434
Watch out! On a sunny day, You mustn't bring an umbrella means you're not allowed to bring one. That's not what we mean!

14
00:01:31.584 --> 00:01:37.170
We mean it's not necessary, so we use don't have to: You don't have to bring an umbrella.

15
00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:43.938
Remember: mustn't means not allowed; don't have to means not necessary.

16
00:01:44.088 --> 00:01:54.378
Your turn! Pupils ___ cross the road at the green man. Must or might? Pause and think.

17
00:01:54.528 --> 00:02:00.762
The answer is must, because it's a safety rule. Did you get it right?

18
00:02:00.912 --> 00:02:11.442
Another one! Take an umbrella. It ___ rain later. May or must? Pause and think.

19
00:02:11.592 --> 00:02:18.450
The answer is may, because rain is only a possibility. Did you get it right?

20
00:02:18.600 --> 00:02:29.994
Try this one! You ___ run in the library. It is a rule. Mustn't or don't have to? Pause and think.

21
00:02:30.144 --> 00:02:40.362
The answer is mustn't. It's a rule, so running is not allowed. Don't have to only means it isn't necessary. Did you get it right?

22
00:02:40.512 --> 00:02:50.778
Last one! He can ___ very fast. Run, runs, or to run? Pause and think.

23
00:02:50.928 --> 00:02:58.122
The answer is run, because a modal is followed by the base verb. Did you get it right?

24
00:02:58.272 --> 00:03:13.074
Remember: can for ability, may for possibility, must for a strong need, should for advice, and would for politeness. A modal is followed by the base verb. Mustn't means not allowed, and don't have to means not necessary.

25
00:03:13.224 --> 00:03:17.658
Can you give a friend some advice using should?

